## Verdict: Redis premise is false

There is **no Redis connection configured** anywhere in `/bench/repo`. A full-repo search for `redis`, `memcached`, and `valkey` returned zero matches. `go.mod` has no Redis client dependency.

The backend cache layer is an **in-process, in-memory TTL cache** in `store/cache/`.

---

## Actual cache implementation

The cache is a thread-safe in-memory store backed by `sync.Map`, with per-entry absolute expiration times and a background cleanup goroutine:

```66:73:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// Cache is a thread-safe in-memory cache with TTL and memory management.
type Cache struct {
	itemCount  atomic.Int64 // Use atomic operations to track item count
	data       sync.Map
	config     Config
	stopChan   chan struct{}
	closedChan chan struct{}
}
```

Construction starts a cleanup loop:

```75:84:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// New creates a new memory cache with the given configuration.
func New(config Config) *Cache {
	c := &Cache{
		config:     config,
		stopChan:   make(chan struct{}),
		closedChan: make(chan struct{}),
	}

	go c.cleanupLoop()
	return c
}
```

Default package-level config (also mirrored at the store layer):

```56:64:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// DefaultConfig returns a default configuration for the cache.
func DefaultConfig() Config {
	return Config{
		DefaultTTL:      10 * time.Minute,
		CleanupInterval: 5 * time.Minute,
		MaxItems:        1000,
		OnEviction:      nil,
	}
}
```

---

## Construction and wiring

`Store` owns three separate cache instances (`instanceSettingCache`, `userCache`, `userSettingCache`), all created with the same config in `store.New`:

```23:30:/bench/repo/store/store.go
	// Cache settings
	cacheConfig cache.Config

	// Caches
	instanceSettingCache *cache.Cache // cache for instance settings
	userCache            *cache.Cache // cache for users
	userSettingCache     *cache.Cache // cache for user settings
```

```37:60:/bench/repo/store/store.go
// New creates a new instance of Store.
func New(driver Driver, profile *profile.Profile) *Store {
	// Default cache settings
	cacheConfig := cache.Config{
		DefaultTTL:      10 * time.Minute,
		CleanupInterval: 5 * time.Minute,
		MaxItems:        1000,
		OnEviction:      nil,
	}

	store := &Store{
		driver:               driver,
		profile:              profile,
		cacheConfig:          cacheConfig,
		instanceSettingCache: cache.New(cacheConfig),
		userCache:            cache.New(cacheConfig),
		userSettingCache:     cache.New(cacheConfig),
		// ...
	}

	return store
}
```

Application entrypoint wires it as:

```64:64:/bench/repo/cmd/memos/main.go
			storeInstance := store.New(dbDriver, instanceProfile)
```

There is also a separate, non-TTL in-memory `deploymentConfiguration` map (for file-backed deployment config), distinct from the `cache.Cache` type:

```20:21:/bench/repo/store/store.go
	deploymentConfigMu sync.RWMutex
	deploymentConfig   *deploymentConfiguration
```

---

## User-entry TTL

User cache entries use the **default TTL of 10 minutes**. User code never calls `SetWithTTL`; it always calls `Set`, which delegates to `DefaultTTL`:

```92:95:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// Set adds a value to the cache with the default TTL.
func (c *Cache) Set(ctx context.Context, key string, value any) {
	c.SetWithTTL(ctx, key, value, c.config.DefaultTTL)
}
```

```97:98:/bench/repo/store/user.go
	s.userCache.Set(ctx, userCacheKey(user.ID), user)
```

Cache keys are the stringified user ID:

```88:90:/bench/repo/store/user.go
func userCacheKey(userID int32) string {
	return strconv.Itoa(int(userID))
}
```

**Population** (all via `Set`, so 10-minute TTL from write time):
- `CreateUser` — ```98:98:/bench/repo/store/user.go```
- `CreateUserIfNoUsers` — ```122:122:/bench/repo/store/user.go```
- `UpdateUser` — ```132:132:/bench/repo/store/user.go```
- `ListUsers` (each user) — ```143:143:/bench/repo/store/user.go```
- `GetUser` (on miss, after DB fetch) — ```167:167:/bench/repo/store/user.go```

**Lookup** (ID-based only):
- `GetUser` — ```150:153:/bench/repo/store/user.go```

**Invalidation**:
- `DeleteUser` → `deleteUserCache` — ```40:40:/bench/repo/store/user_delete.go```

---

## TTL is fixed, not sliding

Expiration is set once at write time:

```110:113:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
	c.data.Store(key, item{
		value:      value,
		expiration: time.Now().Add(ttl),
		size:       size,
```

On read, `Get` only checks expiration; it does **not** extend or reset it:

```122:146:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// Get retrieves a value from the cache.
func (c *Cache) Get(_ context.Context, key string) (any, bool) {
	value, ok := c.data.Load(key)
	if !ok {
		return nil, false
	}

	itm, ok := value.(item)
	// ...
	if time.Now().After(itm.expiration) {
		c.data.Delete(key)
		// ...
		return nil, false
	}

	return itm.value, true
}
```

A cache hit in `GetUser` returns immediately without calling `Set`:

```150:153:/bench/repo/store/user.go
		if cache, ok := s.userCache.Get(ctx, userCacheKey(*find.ID)); ok {
			user, ok := cache.(*User)
			if ok {
				return user, nil
```

So TTL is **absolute from last write**, not sliding on read. A write (create/update/list repopulation) resets the 10-minute window; repeated reads alone do not.

Expired entries are also removed by a periodic background cleanup every 5 minutes (`CleanupInterval`):

```199:214:/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go
// cleanupLoop periodically cleans up expired items.
func (c *Cache) cleanupLoop() {
	ticker := time.NewTicker(c.config.CleanupInterval)
	// ...
	for {
		select {
		case <-ticker.C:
			c.cleanup()
		case <-c.stopChan:
			return
		}
	}
}
```

---

## Summary table

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| Redis connection configured? | **No** — not present in code or dependencies |
| Actual cache | In-process `store/cache.Cache` (`sync.Map` + TTL) |
| Wired where? | `store.New()` → `cmd/memos/main.go:64` |
| User entry TTL | **10 minutes** (`10 * time.Minute`) |
| Sliding/refreshed on read? | **No** — fixed TTL from last `Set`; reads do not refresh |
| Configurable TTL for users? | **No** — hardcoded in `store.New`; no env/config override found |

